meta {
  name: Login
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{senderEmail}}",
    "password": "SuperSecret123!"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.result && res.body.result.token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("jwt_token", res.body.result.token);
  }
}

docs {
  POST /login — mints a real, server-issued JWT (HS256, signed with the
  server's JWT_SECRET, 1h expiry) for the sender account created by
  "Account / Create Account (Sender)". Closes the gap CLAUDE.md used to
  document ("no HTTP login/token-issuance endpoint exists") — Wallet and
  Remittance requests no longer need to forge their own token locally.

  Run "Account / Create Account (Sender)" first so `senderEmail` is
  populated (this reuses that same email + the fixed password used to sign
  it up). Response includes token, userId, accountId, email — the token is
  saved to the `jwt_token` environment variable automatically.

  One login is enough for the whole collection run: authMiddleware only
  checks that the bearer token is a valid, unexpired signature — it doesn't
  verify the token's accountId matches the accountId in the request body —
  so this same token also works for the recipient's Open Wallet call below.

  Wrong password or unknown email → 401 (InvalidCredentialsError, generic
  message — deliberately doesn't reveal which one was wrong).
}
